## 13. BACKEND, DATABASE, AND API INTEGRATION

Connect all major screens to the existing Express backend and Supabase database.

Inspect the current database schema before making changes. Add migrations only where needed and keep them repeatable and safe.

Use appropriate relational models for courses, modules, lessons, enrollments, lesson progress, assessment definitions, assessment attempts, submissions, certificates, and certificate verification.

Enforce foreign keys, uniqueness constraints, validation, and appropriate indexes.

Implement or extend API routes for course discovery, course details, enrollment, lesson access, progress synchronization, assessment attempts, results, certificates, and verification.

Use consistent API response structures and meaningful HTTP status codes.

Validate request bodies and path parameters on the server. Handle missing records, unauthorized requests, invalid input, conflicts, rate limits, and service outages gracefully.

Use transactions or other appropriate consistency mechanisms when multiple database updates must succeed together.

Configure Row Level Security policies and server-side authorization so learners cannot read or modify another learner's private records.

Do not place privileged credentials in frontend code. Do not disable security controls to make a demonstration appear functional.

Keep database errors in server logs where appropriate, but return safe, useful messages to users.

## 14. ACCESSIBILITY, RESPONSIVENESS, AND USABILITY

Meet practical accessibility expectations throughout the application.

Use semantic HTML, correctly associated form labels, keyboard navigation, visible focus indicators, meaningful button labels, and accessible dialogs.

Make error messages available to screen readers. Use appropriate live regions for changing status messages.

Ensure interactive targets are easy to operate on mobile. Maintain readable text, suitable contrast, and layouts that adapt to small screens.

Avoid relying solely on hover effects or color to communicate important information.

Respect reduced-motion preferences and provide clear loading, empty, success, and error states.

Use consistent button styles and predictable interaction patterns.

Keep the learning experience simple enough for a first-time user to understand without extensive instructions.

## 15. HACKATHON DEMONSTRATION AND SEED DATA

Prepare a complete, coherent demonstration journey for judges.

A judge should be able to:

1. Open the landing page and discover a course.
2. Review its syllabus as a guest.
3. Register or sign in.
4. Enroll in the course.
5. Open a module and study a lesson.
6. Complete a practice activity.
7. See progress saved.
8. Disconnect from the internet and revisit eligible cached content.
9. Reconnect and observe pending progress synchronize.
10. Complete an online formal assessment.
11. Review the result.
12. Satisfy the configured completion requirements.
13. Generate and download a completion certificate.
14. Open its public verification page.
15. Review achievements in the Skill Passport.

Provide realistic seed data for a small set of courses, modules, lessons, and assessments across relevant skills such as web development, programming, data analysis, and digital productivity.

Ensure that seeded content is internally consistent and that the completion requirements can actually be met during a demonstration.

Do not hardcode successful login responses, pretend to submit data to Supabase, or display fabricated learner progress as if it came from a real account.

If a demo learner feature already exists, inspect its implementation and ensure it cannot bypass authorization or expose privileged credentials.

